1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,689
In its last financial year, Costco sold two hundred

2
00:00:03,689 --> 00:00:06,944
and ninety-seven billion dollars' worth of stuff.

3
00:00:06,944 --> 00:00:12,260
Groceries, TVs, tires, gasoline, and a very large number of rotisserie chickens.

4
00:00:12,710 --> 00:00:17,146
After paying for all those goods, and for every warehouse, every worker and

5
00:00:17,146 --> 00:00:21,819
every truck, about five point eight billion dollars were left from those sales.

6
00:00:21,819 --> 00:00:23,830
Less than two cents on the dollar.

7
00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:26,789
Then there's a second line in the accounts.

8
00:00:26,789 --> 00:00:28,656
Five point nine billion dollars,

9
00:00:28,656 --> 00:00:32,040
from eighty-four million people paying for a plastic card.

10
00:00:32,490 --> 00:00:36,039
Those fees are about two percent of Costco's revenue.

11
00:00:36,039 --> 00:00:38,650
And about half of its operating profit.

12
00:00:39,100 --> 00:00:41,430
So here's the claim we're going to test.

13
00:00:41,430 --> 00:00:44,169
Costco isn't really a store that charges a fee.

14
00:00:44,169 --> 00:00:47,140
It's a membership that comes with a store attached.

15
00:00:47,590 --> 00:00:51,310
Let's take the receipt apart, line by line.

16
00:00:54,910 --> 00:00:59,250
Costco's financial year ended on August thirtieth, 2026.

17
00:00:59,250 --> 00:01:03,512
Total revenue: three hundred and three billion dollars.

18
00:01:03,512 --> 00:01:08,085
Almost all of it is what Costco calls net sales, the goods.

19
00:01:08,085 --> 00:01:12,270
The rest, five point nine billion, is membership fees.

20
00:01:12,720 --> 00:01:15,640
Now follow one hundred dollars spent at the checkout.

21
00:01:16,090 --> 00:01:20,142
About eighty-nine dollars go straight back to the companies that made the goods.

22
00:01:20,142 --> 00:01:22,370
That's what Costco paid for the merchandise.

23
00:01:22,820 --> 00:01:27,553
Another nine dollars and fifteen cents pay for running everything else:

24
00:01:27,553 --> 00:01:31,220
wages, warehouses, electricity, card fees, head office.

25
00:01:31,670 --> 00:01:36,350
What's left from the sale itself: one dollar and ninety-four cents.

26
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:38,239
Now add the membership.

27
00:01:38,239 --> 00:01:40,428
For every hundred dollars of sales,

28
00:01:40,428 --> 00:01:43,556
members also pay Costco about two dollars in fees.

29
00:01:43,556 --> 00:01:47,560
And unlike a sale, a fee doesn't come with any goods to pay for.

30
00:01:48,010 --> 00:01:51,839
Put the two together, and Costco makes about three dollars and

31
00:01:51,839 --> 00:01:56,348
ninety-three cents of operating profit on every hundred dollars of sales.

32
00:01:56,348 --> 00:02:00,610
After interest and taxes, it keeps about three dollars and ten cents.

33
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:06,183
Over the whole year, that's eleven point seven billion dollars of operating profit.

34
00:02:06,183 --> 00:02:08,961
Roughly half came from what's on the shelves.

35
00:02:08,961 --> 00:02:11,060
The other half came from the card.

36
00:02:11,510 --> 00:02:15,097
For comparison, American food retailers keep about two cents

37
00:02:15,097 --> 00:02:18,684
of net profit per dollar, according to their industry group.

38
00:02:18,684 --> 00:02:19,939
So Costco isn't poor.

39
00:02:19,939 --> 00:02:22,510
It just makes its money in a strange place.

40
00:02:22,960 --> 00:02:25,543
Which raises the question: why would a store

41
00:02:25,543 --> 00:02:28,360
choose to make so little on the things it sells?

42
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:32,356
On everything it sells, Costco's gross margin

43
00:02:32,356 --> 00:02:35,124
was eleven point oh nine percent last year.

44
00:02:35,124 --> 00:02:39,501
That's the slice left after paying suppliers, before any other cost.

45
00:02:39,501 --> 00:02:43,942
Costco's own annual report says its volume lets it operate profitably

46
00:02:43,942 --> 00:02:47,740
at "significantly lower gross margins" than most retailers.

47
00:02:48,190 --> 00:02:49,744
That's not an accident.

48
00:02:49,744 --> 00:02:53,594
For decades, Costco has been reported to cap its markups:

49
00:02:53,594 --> 00:02:59,066
around fourteen percent on national brands, and fifteen percent on its own brand.

50
00:02:59,066 --> 00:03:01,634
It's not written in the annual report.

51
00:03:01,634 --> 00:03:03,390
But the eleven percent is.

52
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:06,334
That own brand is Kirkland Signature.

53
00:03:06,334 --> 00:03:10,715
Costco's finance chief says Kirkland products are priced at least

54
00:03:10,715 --> 00:03:14,894
fifteen to twenty percent below the national brand equivalent.

55
00:03:14,894 --> 00:03:19,680
Store brands made up just under a third of Costco's sales in late 2024.

56
00:03:20,130 --> 00:03:24,493
And when Costco's own costs fall, it tends to pass the saving on.

57
00:03:24,493 --> 00:03:27,849
On its latest earnings call, it listed price cuts:

58
00:03:27,849 --> 00:03:32,145
Kirkland walnuts from thirteen seventy-nine to nine ninety-nine.

59
00:03:32,145 --> 00:03:35,770
Black pepper from six ninety-nine to five ninety-nine.

60
00:03:36,220 --> 00:03:39,123
Then there's the most famous price in retail.

61
00:03:39,123 --> 00:03:42,477
A hot dog and a soda for one dollar and fifty cents.

62
00:03:42,477 --> 00:03:43,896
Same price since 1985.

63
00:03:43,896 --> 00:03:48,540
If it had followed inflation, it would cost about four sixty-five today.

64
00:03:48,990 --> 00:03:54,217
In March 2026, Costco's chief executive, Ron Vachris, was asked about it.

65
00:03:54,217 --> 00:03:59,230
His answer: "The hot dog price will not change as long as I'm around."

66
00:03:59,680 --> 00:04:02,838
Why would a company with three hundred billion

67
00:04:02,838 --> 00:04:05,447
dollars of sales care about a hot dog?

68
00:04:05,447 --> 00:04:10,184
Because at Costco, a price isn't only a way to make money on an item.

69
00:04:10,184 --> 00:04:12,586
It's an advertisement for the card.

70
00:04:12,586 --> 00:04:15,470
Every member who sees a one-fifty hot dog,

71
00:04:15,470 --> 00:04:19,520
or a cheaper bag of walnuts, gets one more reason to renew.

72
00:04:19,970 --> 00:04:25,141
A normal retailer that finds a cheaper supplier keeps the difference as profit.

73
00:04:25,141 --> 00:04:26,908
Costco mostly passes it on.

74
00:04:26,908 --> 00:04:31,294
In its finance chief's words: "Our goal is to be the first to lower

75
00:04:31,294 --> 00:04:35,417
prices where we see opportunities to do so." A member who feels

76
00:04:35,417 --> 00:04:39,410
the savings is worth more than a few extra cents on one item.

77
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:42,460
But selling at eleven percent margins only works if

78
00:04:42,460 --> 00:04:45,060
you can also run a store for less than anyone else.

79
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:48,644
Here's the first trick.

80
00:04:48,644 --> 00:04:49,400
Choice.

81
00:04:49,850 --> 00:04:55,203
An average American supermarket carries about thirty-three thousand different items.

82
00:04:55,203 --> 00:04:58,452
A Costco warehouse is more than three times bigger,

83
00:04:58,452 --> 00:05:00,810
and carries fewer than four thousand.

84
00:05:01,260 --> 00:05:04,744
Think one or two brands of ketchup, not twenty.

85
00:05:04,744 --> 00:05:05,930
In a giant pack.

86
00:05:05,930 --> 00:05:06,820
On a pallet.

87
00:05:07,270 --> 00:05:09,056
That does a lot of work.

88
00:05:09,056 --> 00:05:12,999
With so few items, each one sells in enormous volume.

89
00:05:12,999 --> 00:05:17,016
Costco's annual report says it plainly: those volumes,

90
00:05:17,016 --> 00:05:22,670
and how fast the goods turn over, are what let it live on such thin margins.

91
00:05:22,670 --> 00:05:26,762
Fewer items also means bigger orders for each supplier,

92
00:05:26,762 --> 00:05:30,110
and fewer, bigger things to unload and stock.

93
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:34,532
The result is a staggering amount of sales per building.

94
00:05:34,532 --> 00:05:38,220
Costco runs nine hundred and thirty-nine warehouses.

95
00:05:38,220 --> 00:05:41,269
Divide its sales by that, and each one does

96
00:05:41,269 --> 00:05:44,603
more than three hundred million dollars a year.

97
00:05:44,603 --> 00:05:48,645
Its closest rival, Walmart's Sam's Club, runs six hundred

98
00:05:48,645 --> 00:05:52,759
and one clubs, with ninety-three billion dollars of sales.

99
00:05:52,759 --> 00:05:56,518
That's about one hundred and fifty-five million each.

100
00:05:56,518 --> 00:05:57,440
Roughly half.

101
00:05:57,890 --> 00:06:00,294
Here's the part that surprises people.

102
00:06:00,294 --> 00:06:03,330
Costco isn't cheap because it pays people badly.

103
00:06:03,780 --> 00:06:08,087
It has about three hundred and fifty-five thousand employees.

104
00:06:08,087 --> 00:06:12,746
In the US, the average hourly wage is around thirty-three dollars.

105
00:06:12,746 --> 00:06:17,900
In early 2025, it set its lowest starting rate at twenty dollars an hour.

106
00:06:18,350 --> 00:06:21,631
And yet all its running costs, wages included,

107
00:06:21,631 --> 00:06:25,198
come to just nine point one five percent of sales.

108
00:06:25,198 --> 00:06:31,190
Not because each worker is cheap, but because each worker moves so much merchandise.

109
00:06:31,640 --> 00:06:35,079
So the shelves make a little money, in a very efficient way.

110
00:06:35,079 --> 00:06:37,200
Now for the line that makes the rest.

111
00:06:38,300 --> 00:06:43,706
At the end of August 2026, Costco had eighty-four point one million paid members.

112
00:06:43,706 --> 00:06:45,776
With the extra household cards,

113
00:06:45,776 --> 00:06:49,580
that's one hundred and fifty million people carrying one.

114
00:06:50,030 --> 00:06:54,137
In the US, a basic membership costs sixty-five dollars a year.

115
00:06:54,137 --> 00:06:58,509
About half of paid members pay double that for the Executive card,

116
00:06:58,509 --> 00:07:02,683
one hundred and thirty dollars, which gives back two percent on

117
00:07:02,683 --> 00:07:06,790
most purchases, up to twelve hundred and fifty dollars a year.

118
00:07:07,240 --> 00:07:09,470
Now the number that matters most.

119
00:07:09,470 --> 00:07:14,200
In the US and Canada, ninety-two point three percent of members renew.

120
00:07:14,650 --> 00:07:16,610
Think about what that means.

121
00:07:16,610 --> 00:07:20,880
Almost every year, more than nine in ten customers pay again,

122
00:07:20,880 --> 00:07:23,750
up front, before they've bought anything.

123
00:07:23,750 --> 00:07:26,480
Costco can count that money in advance,

124
00:07:26,480 --> 00:07:30,330
and it spreads it evenly over the year in its accounts.

125
00:07:30,780 --> 00:07:32,154
Costco says it openly.

126
00:07:32,154 --> 00:07:35,089
In its latest earnings call, Ron Vachris called

127
00:07:35,089 --> 00:07:37,900
membership "the most important item we sell".

128
00:07:38,350 --> 00:07:41,962
And like any product, it can get a price increase.

129
00:07:41,962 --> 00:07:47,163
In September 2024, Costco raised its fees for the first time since 2017:

130
00:07:47,163 --> 00:07:50,559
sixty to sixty-five dollars for the basic card,

131
00:07:50,559 --> 00:07:55,110
one hundred and twenty to one hundred and thirty for Executive.

132
00:07:55,560 --> 00:07:59,980
Over the past year, membership income grew eleven percent.

133
00:07:59,980 --> 00:08:01,809
And renewal didn't fall.

134
00:08:01,809 --> 00:08:02,800
It ticked up.

135
00:08:03,250 --> 00:08:07,003
The benefit from that increase is now fully in the numbers.

136
00:08:07,003 --> 00:08:11,393
In the last quarter, leaving aside the increase and currency effects,

137
00:08:11,393 --> 00:08:15,082
fee income still grew almost seven percent, simply because

138
00:08:15,082 --> 00:08:19,090
there are more members, and more of them on the expensive card.

139
00:08:19,540 --> 00:08:21,791
And the members are getting younger.

140
00:08:21,791 --> 00:08:25,104
According to Ron Vachris, the number of members under

141
00:08:25,104 --> 00:08:28,605
forty has grown nearly sixty percent since the pandemic.

142
00:08:28,605 --> 00:08:31,356
They're now more than a quarter of the base.

143
00:08:31,356 --> 00:08:35,420
A thirty-year-old who renews today could keep paying for decades.

144
00:08:35,870 --> 00:08:38,291
Here's the deal, then, in one sentence.

145
00:08:38,291 --> 00:08:42,141
Members pay Costco for the right to buy things at almost cost.

146
00:08:42,141 --> 00:08:45,742
And Costco has to keep those prices low, every single day,

147
00:08:45,742 --> 00:08:48,350
or that ninety-two percent starts to slip.

148
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:51,554
That's the real discipline behind the hot dog.

149
00:08:51,554 --> 00:08:55,386
Raise prices on the shelves, and you put the membership at risk.

150
00:08:55,386 --> 00:08:58,200
And the membership is where half the profit is.

151
00:08:59,300 --> 00:09:03,860
Two parts of Costco are growing faster than the rest, and both serve the card.

152
00:09:04,310 --> 00:09:05,373
First, gasoline.

153
00:09:05,373 --> 00:09:09,428
Costco ran seven hundred and seventy-four gas stations at the

154
00:09:09,428 --> 00:09:13,549
end of its year, and fuel made up eleven percent of its sales.

155
00:09:13,549 --> 00:09:17,737
When prices at the pump jumped in 2026, drivers came in droves:

156
00:09:17,737 --> 00:09:22,190
in the last quarter, gas sales were up by more than thirty percent.

157
00:09:22,640 --> 00:09:25,990
Costco doesn't say how much it earns on fuel.

158
00:09:25,990 --> 00:09:28,819
But it says gas brings in new members,

159
00:09:28,819 --> 00:09:33,360
and that members who fill up there tend to spend more inside.

160
00:09:33,810 --> 00:09:34,859
Second, online.

161
00:09:34,859 --> 00:09:38,356
Costco's digital sales passed thirty-three billion

162
00:09:38,356 --> 00:09:41,643
dollars last year, up more than twenty percent.

163
00:09:41,643 --> 00:09:45,210
Same model, just without the walk past the pallets.

164
00:09:45,660 --> 00:09:48,636
And the machine is still running hot.

165
00:09:48,636 --> 00:09:53,380
In September 2026, Costco's sales were up thirteen percent.

166
00:09:53,380 --> 00:09:57,241
Without the effect of gas prices and currencies,

167
00:09:57,241 --> 00:10:00,940
comparable sales were up almost eight percent.

168
00:10:01,390 --> 00:10:03,507
What about the competition?

169
00:10:03,507 --> 00:10:08,994
In its annual report, Costco lists Walmart, Target, Kroger and Amazon,

170
00:10:08,994 --> 00:10:13,070
plus the other warehouse clubs, Sam's Club and BJ's.

171
00:10:13,520 --> 00:10:16,193
Several of them run the same playbook.

172
00:10:16,193 --> 00:10:21,540
Walmart says membership income is a significant part of Sam's Club's profit.

173
00:10:21,540 --> 00:10:24,565
And Amazon Prime is another way of charging

174
00:10:24,565 --> 00:10:27,520
shoppers up front, so that they come back.

175
00:10:27,970 --> 00:10:30,411
Costco's answer is to keep building.

176
00:10:30,411 --> 00:10:33,395
It opened twenty-eight warehouses last year,

177
00:10:33,395 --> 00:10:38,685
and plans thirty-three more this year, including in Europe, Canada and Mexico.

178
00:10:38,685 --> 00:10:42,890
Every new building is a new pool of people who can buy a card.

179
00:10:43,340 --> 00:10:47,492
So the real fight isn't over who sells the cheapest ketchup.

180
00:10:47,492 --> 00:10:52,820
It's over which membership people decide is worth paying for again next year.

181
00:10:53,920 --> 00:10:55,760
So, how does Costco make money?

182
00:10:56,210 --> 00:10:57,942
On the goods, barely.

183
00:10:57,942 --> 00:11:01,899
About two dollars on every hundred, after costs.

184
00:11:01,899 --> 00:11:03,466
On the card, a lot.

185
00:11:03,466 --> 00:11:09,650
Almost six billion dollars a year, for a product that doesn't need a shelf.

186
00:11:10,100 --> 00:11:12,897
That doesn't mean the shelves don't matter.

187
00:11:12,897 --> 00:11:17,125
Without the cheap walnuts, the pallets and the one-fifty hot dog,

188
00:11:17,125 --> 00:11:20,183
nobody would pay sixty-five dollars to walk in.

189
00:11:20,183 --> 00:11:21,874
The shelves are the proof.

190
00:11:21,874 --> 00:11:23,500
The card is the business.

191
00:11:23,950 --> 00:11:26,074
And the lesson goes beyond Costco.

192
00:11:26,074 --> 00:11:30,010
The cheapest thing in the building is often there for a reason.

193
00:11:30,010 --> 00:11:34,071
Sometimes the price on the shelf isn't how a company makes money.

194
00:11:34,071 --> 00:11:37,070
It's how it convinces you to pay somewhere else.
